Construct choice values through the choice name and variant name.

### Unit variants

A unit variant is a value without a constructor call.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    current = Status::Ready
]

This form is invalid:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- INVALID: unit variants are not constructor calls
    current = Status::Ready()
]

### Payload variants

A payload variant requires constructor parentheses.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    failed = Status::Failed("network")
    named = Status::Failed(message = "network")
]

### Argument rules

- Positional arguments fill payload fields in declaration order.
- Positional arguments must come before named arguments.
- A named argument must match a payload field.
- Each payload field must be supplied exactly once.
- Payload fields have no defaults.
- Extra or missing arguments are invalid.
- Argument values are checked and contextually coerced against field types.

An immediate `Route of ...` parameter supplies one bounded exception to complete
construction: a direct constructor may capture a leading payload prefix and
leave the trailing field for its future input. The ordinary rules above still
apply outside that boundary. The @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/docs/src/docs/wiring/wiring.mtf (Wiring reference)
owns the exact zero/one-input contract, capture order and forwarding limits.

Constructed payload fields are immutable.

Direct payload field mutation is invalid.

Use pattern matching to inspect payload fields.

### Qualified variant headers

Write qualified variants as `Status::Ready`. The separator touches both names:
no whitespace, line break or comment may appear on either side of `::`.
Whitespace before `::`, or any gap after it, reports `MOTH-SYNTAX-0031`; a line
break before `::` ends the expression first. The spaced `Status :: Ready, Busy;`
form is choice declaration syntax and does not apply to construction.

### Variant construction MON data boundary

Source construction keeps the explicit `Choice::Variant` form above; contextual `::Variant` source construction is deferred. MON literal data documents the same unit/payload distinction with either contextual `::Variant` or one exact `Choice::Variant` qualifier. The receiving schema selects the expected choice, and an explicit qualifier is a checked assertion that never selects another schema. Matching payload shapes do not make nominal choices interchangeable, and payload fields still have no defaults. That contract is Rust-only serialised-data documentation. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).
